[{:seon.task/name "a-platform-error-closes-when-its-cause-is-gone"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-25 on the standing root (main): an open :platform problem `:seon.watch/died` (its batch handler threw: docs/references.md, No such file or directory), count 1, first and last ms 1792758464909, from an earlier boot. Root's own boot b6569825 runs a watcher that is alive: its `:seon.root/watch` fact has :boot b6569825, 11 batches and no :error. Reproduction (root's REPL): `(let [db @(:conn sys)] {:watch (:seon.root/watch (datahike.api/pull db [:seon.root/watch] [:seon.root/id :root])) :died (->> (my.root/problems sys) :classes :platform :rows (filter #(= :seon.watch/died (:type %))))})` answers a live watch fact and a died entry that is not `:open? false`. Cause: `seon.problems/closed?` closes a recorded error only when a later fact is written on its `:seon.error/subject`, a var's name or a section's address. `my.root/watch!`'s `died` records the error by `seon.root/platform-tx` with no subject, so nothing can ever close it: the next boot's watcher writes its fresh `:seon.root/watch` fact, which is the cause being gone, and the reader cannot see it. Correct, at the producer (the existing close rule, no second one): the error names what would show its cause gone as its subject, and `closed?` resolves it as it resolves the others. For the watcher that is root's `:seon.root/watch` fact. The died write lands in the same transaction as the error, so it is not later; only a watcher that started, or a batch it loaded, writes that fact afterwards. That needs `:seon.error/subject` to name an attribute of an entity, e.g. `[[:seon.root/id :root] :seon.root/watch]`, closed when that attribute has a datom after the error's last tx (`d/datoms db :eavt e a`). It is a format change, so it waits for root's gate (§3's four questions). Alternative: the watcher's fact becomes its own entity, `{:seon.root/id :watch}`, so the existing entity subject works with no schema change. Its cost is one more entity, and every root write site of the watch fact moves to it. Recommended: the alternative, since nothing else is written on that entity, so a later datom means a live watcher, and it adds no subject form. The same rule then serves case 4's `:health` task (`seon.reconcile/root-work-is-tasks`), which closes when its problem closes. The same holds for a task file refused (docs/failure-points.md item 8): seon.problems/closed? closes an error only through :seon.error/subject, and seon.task/file-refused names none, so \"task file my.task.edn: not readable EDN\" stays open after the file reads again. The refused file's error names that file as its subject, which closed? reads."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.watch-test/a-dead-watchers-error-closes-when-a-new-watcher-writes-its-fact}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-section-goes-stale-when-what-it-cites-changes-meaning"
  :seon.task/plan "Waits on the owner's ruling. ```seon.problems/stale-sections``` makes a (section, var) pair stale when the cited var's form digest differs from the one recorded when the section was written, so any edit of a widely cited var stales every section citing it: main shows 223, mostly code churn rather than wrong prose (the archived paper dogfood/docs-audit.md (git history)). Proposed: a section goes stale when a cited var's contract (:malli/schema) or docstring changes, or the var is removed; a body-only change does not. First split main's 223 by kind (absent then, digest changed, no baseline) and count how many each rule keeps."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/a-body-change-does-not-stale-a-section seon.problems-test/a-contract-change-stales-a-section}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-problem-row-holds-what-it-knows"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the printer audit (docs/render.md; owner, 2026-10-25: \"focus on not generating crap and then not rendering crap\"). An entry of ```seon.problems/health``` carries keys that say nothing, required by :seon.problems/row: `:producer nil`, `:at-ms nil`, `:proofs []`, `:answered-by []` on every derived entry (```seon.problems/derived-row```, the entry function in ```seon.problems/dead-citations```), and repeats itself: a recorded error's entry (```seon.problems/error-row```) holds :example, :signature, :count and :agents both at its top and in :evidence; a dead citation's :evidence repeats its :name and :section. Printed raw (a value that lost its schema's metadata, e.g. (:classes (my.root/problems sys))), the 177 :stale-section entries were 13 keys each, most of them empty. Correct: an entry holds a key only when it knows its value (the schema's keys optional, nil meaning unknown never stored), and :evidence only what the entry's own keys do not say; its readers (the done and merge gates, seon.render.ai/problem-data, seon.render.ui/problem-row) read a missing key as none. Proof: every entry of health on a seeded db has no nil or empty value and no :evidence key equal to its own."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/a-problem-row-holds-what-it-knows}}
 {:seon.task/name "closed-names-no-legacy-subject"
  :seon.task/plan "seon.problems/closed? carries a fallback for :seon.doc/refused entries recorded before errors named a subject (the link's target and the citing section's address, read off the example). Every producer names :seon.error/subject now (ff68a7b630, 00d1d3fda3: task files :seon.root/tasks, source reads :seon.root/source; seon.error/asked-tx). Delete the fallback, with a test that every recorded error class names a subject at its producer. Two cases: if runs/store is erased (the owner approved it at the flaky-results merge; it may be skipped now, since that change did not need it), no entry without a subject remains and the deletion is all; if it is not erased, the standing store still holds entries without a subject that only the fallback or nothing closes (at least db 1795, task file my.task.edn not readable EDN, and db 1979, git add -A unable to stat the archived paper root-handoff-2026-10-25.md (git history), both recorded before the subjects), so first retract those signature entities on the owner's go (one write through seon_eval root), then delete the fallback."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "problems-costs-what-is-open"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-30: (my.root/problems sys) on the standing root (main d85a91aed1) allocated 597 MB and 215M ticks in 472 ms to answer 2 problems; on the md-scan bench root it passed the 1 GiB form budget and was stopped (seon.repl/bytes-spent), so a session asking for root's problems got none. seon.problems/health reads main whole each call. A report of what is open should cost what is open and what changed since it was last drawn (docs/render.md: a view is a render of what exists, at the cost of what changed), not main's size. Plan: measure which classes cost (seon.profile/measure! per class of health), then read each from its index (a problem is a fact with its class; the derived classes, :dead-citation and the like, are computed when their inputs change, by the loads that make them true: seon.problems/caused already runs per load) and draw the report from the facts. Cost proof: problems' bytes flat in main's size, linear in open problems."
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-measure-past-its-bound-is-a-problem"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03: \"If we put in bounds we could have problems detect themselves. The point is to passively update it progressively as the system runs rather than do a poll of the current system\"; and before, on health: \"We are supposed to be passively collecting data and problems\" (agent evals, turn times, new agents). What exists: the measures are recorded as facts at their writes: an eval's :ms :ticks :bytes (in :seon.eval/response, seon.repl/record-tx), a model call's :ms, tokens, cache hit and miss tokens and its split (:seon.model-call/*), an agent's turn (:seon.agent/turn), a task's outcome and waste (:seon.task/outcome, :seon.task/waste, waste only at the end), a process's forked and ended ms and mem-mb (:seon.process/*). Two measures already become a recorded problem at their write: :seon.turn/cache-miss (seon.turn) and :seon.process/stall (seon.problems/stalled). The rest have no bound, so nothing judges them: on the standing root (2026-11-03) one eval took 2.7 GB of bytes and nothing was open. Health (seon.problems/health) lists problems only, no measures; an agent's context (seon.context's work view) shows its proofs, the done rule's reasons and the problems its branch caused (seon.problems/caused-split), not its measures against any bound; the model-call facts stay on the agent's branch, so main holds none (0 on the standing root after a green 3-turn run). The change, one mechanism: (1) each bound is a config key in resources/seon/config/default.edn under seon.config.*, set from measured costs (memory: limits fail loudly; no made-up limits): an eval's ticks and bytes, a run's turns and tokens, a model call's cache hit share, a process's mem-mb, an agent's wait; wall time is measured and shown, never judged (AGENTS section 5, cost). (2) The writer of each measure compares it with its bound at the write and records a problem fact holding the measure and the bound, as cache-miss does: one comparison a write, nothing on an idle system, no poll and no proc. Generalize cache-miss and stall into it rather than beside them. (3) Health reads them as a recorded class; an agent's context shows the open ones on its own branch on its next turn; root's render shows all. (4) Aggregates (turns to green, agents started, tokens a task, the distribution of eval cost) are a render over the facts, drawn on read at the cost of what changed since its t (derived data cached by t, never relied on), for the dashboard and a root agent's context. (5) The run's measures reach main: the model-call facts (or their per-run sums) merge with the run, so main's aggregates see every run. Measure first: the bounds come from the distributions on a run of real tasks. Docs: docs/health.md (a measure past its bound), docs/cost.md, docs/dashboard.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/an-eval-past-its-bytes-bound-opens-a-problem seon.problems-test/a-runs-measures-reach-main}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-names-change"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-05: \"Name changes too. I don't want these old ideas to come back and haunt us again\"; keep the docs and tasks updated rather than a list of what not to say. In one merge after the code they name is settled: component for part, unit and tile; ``agent-view`` for render-ctx; ``agent-messages`` for the agent's model render; render tree for walk and walker; ``conversation``, ``turn``, ``eval-record``, ``message``, ``task``, ``cite``, ``turn-line``, ``problems``, ``proofs`` for conversation-list, chat-turn, eval-item and evaluation, chat-message, task-data, cited, run-head, own-problems and status; ``start-work`` for begin-run!; element ids by what they hold (c-rows, p-task, p-tiles, p-head, c-side); test names and :as :row, :rows, fill-rows, form-row and the like with them. Sweeps the 160 of 402 open tasks written on what has gone: rewritten as things now are, or removed when what they plan is gone. Removes jolt/render-core, jolt/insert-and-run, jolt/one-render-6-the-page-is-the-last-message and the worktrees of finished experiments once their good work has merged (a store is the owner's to reclaim). Proofs: bin/prove green; every doc and open task reads in today's names."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.context/teaching-is-data"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}]
